  "final_answer": "I now have all the information needed to answer the question. Let me summarize my findings.\n\n## Task Summary: Comparing Private Repositories in GitHub's Free and Pro Plans\n\n### Key Finding: GitHub Has Removed the \"Pro\" Plan\n\nAfter thoroughly exploring GitHub's pricing page (https://github.com/pricing), I found that **GitHub has removed the \"Pro\" plan** from their current pricing structure.\n\n### Current GitHub Plans (as observed):\nGitHub's pricing page now lists only **three plans**:\n\n| Plan | Price (per user/month) | Private Repositories |\n|------|------------------------|----------------------|\n| **Free** | $0 | **Unlimited** |\n| **Team** | $4 | **Unlimited** |\n| **Enterprise** | $21 | **Unlimited** |\n\n### Comparison Results:\n\n- **Free Plan**: Allows **Unlimited** private repositories\n- **Pro Plan**: ⚠️ **Does not exist on GitHub's current pricing page**\n\n### Evidence:\n1. The main pricing page (https://github.com/pricing) only shows Free, Team, and Enterprise plans.\n2. The \"Compare all features\" table on the same pricing page only has columns for Free, Team, and Enterprise (no Pro column).\n3. The URL https://github.com/pricing/pro returns a 404 error, confirming the Pro plan is no longer available.\n4. All current plans (Free, Team, and Enterprise) show \"Unlimited\" for private repositories.\n\n### Conclusion:\nA comparison between Free and Pro plans is not possible because **GitHub has discontinued the Pro plan**. Under the current pricing structure, the Free plan already includes unlimited private repositories, which was historically a key feature that distinguished the Pro plan from the Free plan. If you were looking for information about a specific plan, you may want to verify the plan name, as GitHub's current tier structure is Free → Team → Enterprise.",
